Just re-reading the Odyssey, and it seems to me the ancient Greeks
used to sacrifice animals to their Gods *after* an event to offer
thanks, as well as immediately before the event, to ask for help with
it.

For example they would offer sacrifices before a voyage to ask for
good weather and safe journey, and if they got it they would offer
more sacrifices upon arrival.

Now, I'm sure someone will point out that NHINAG, but I daresay
nethack's sacrifices owe at least something to the ancient Greek
traditions, and I imagine it wasn't the only sacrifice-culture to use
a similar system of 'please' and 'thankyou'.

What I'm saying is, perhaps in nethack there should be a short period
after each gift/golden glow/contented stomach etc in which a player
gets extra brownie points for sacrificing, with some sort of message
like "[Deity] accepts your thanks, you have a hopeful feeling."

Perhaps there could even be some kind of penalty (increased prayer
timeout?) if you consistently fail to give thanks after getting
something nice from your god. Of course this might be unfair in games
where you haven't yet found an altar, but I'm sure we could work
something out.

